issues.problematic_code_snippet:
// Ensure we're authenticated before proceeding with sign-out
await Task.Yield();

var state = await AuthenticationProvider.GetAuthenticationStateAsync();

private async Task ProcessLogOut(string returnUrl)
{
    if (!ValidateSignOutRequestState())
    {
        Log.LogoutOperationInitiatedExternally(Logger);
        Navigation.NavigateTo(ApplicationPaths.LogOutFailedPath, AuthenticationNavigationOptions with { HistoryEntryState = "The logout was not initiated from within the page." });
        return;
    }

public void Redirect()
{
    if (_tokenResult.InteractionOptions != null)
    {
        _navigation.NavigateToLogin(_tokenResult.InteractiveRequestUrl, _tokenResult.InteractionOptions);
    }
    else
    {
        _navigation.NavigateTo(_tokenResult.InteractiveRequestUrl!);
    }

findings.body:
The new check rejects every logout that arrives without `HistoryEntryState`:

::diff{file="src/Components/WebAssembly/WebAssembly.Authentication/src/RemoteAuthenticatorViewCore.cs" lines="279-292"}

`ValidateSignOutRequestState()` returns `GetCachedNavigationState()?.Interaction == InteractionType.SignOut`, and `GetCachedNavigationState()` returns `null` when `HistoryEntryState` is empty. So a null state means validation fails and the user is sent to `logout-failed`.

The existing test `AuthenticationManager_Logout_NavigatesToDefaultReturnUrlWhenNoReturnUrlIsPresent` (RemoteAuthenticatorCoreTests.cs ~L317) calls `CreateAuthenticationManager("https://www.example.com/base/authentication/logout")` with `currentState = null`. It expects `.../authentication/logged-out`. That only passed because the old code fell through to `TestSignOutSessionStateManager.ValidateSignOutState()`, which returned `true`. The PR removes that test helper, so the test will now land on `logout-failed` and fail.

::callout{type="warning"}
The PR description says the new logic will "only fail validation when state exists but is invalid". The code does the opposite: it fails when no state exists. Rejecting state-less logouts is probably the right CSRF behaviour now that the sessionStorage fallback is gone, but the description and the tests need to match it.

To keep the stricter behaviour, update the test so it passes a SignOut state without a `ReturnUrl`:

::suggestion{file="src/Components/WebAssembly/WebAssembly.Authentication/test/RemoteAuthenticatorCoreTests.cs" lines="320-321"}
        var (remoteAuthenticator, renderer, authServiceMock) = CreateAuthenticationManager(
            "https://www.example.com/base/authentication/logout",
            new InteractiveRequestOptions { Interaction = InteractionType.SignOut, ReturnUrl = null }.ToState());

Also add a test that asserts a logout with a null `HistoryEntryState` redirects to `logout-failed`.

This was added to `ProcessLogOut`, which otherwise only had obsolete code removed:

::diff{file="src/Components/WebAssembly/WebAssembly.Authentication/src/RemoteAuthenticatorViewCore.cs" lines="288-293"}

The comment says "Ensure we're authenticated before proceeding with sign-out", but `Task.Yield()` doesn't check authentication. It only posts the continuation back onto the renderer's dispatcher. That adds an extra async hop in the middle of a security-relevant flow, which can let other renderer work interleave between `AuthenticationState.ReturnUrl = returnUrl` and the sign-out call. It also has nothing to do with removing obsolete APIs. The actual authentication check is the `GetAuthenticationStateAsync()` call on the next line.

::suggestion{file="src/Components/WebAssembly/WebAssembly.Authentication/src/RemoteAuthenticatorViewCore.cs" lines="288-293"}
        AuthenticationState.ReturnUrl = returnUrl;

        var state = await AuthenticationProvider.GetAuthenticationStateAsync();

Before this PR, `NavigateToLogin` was only called when both `InteractionOptions` and `InteractiveRequestUrl` were non-null:

::diff{file="src/Components/WebAssembly/WebAssembly.Authentication/src/Services/AccessTokenNotAvailableException.cs" lines="36-46"}

Now the first branch passes `string? InteractiveRequestUrl` to `NavigateToLogin(this NavigationManager, string loginPath, InteractiveRequestOptions)`, whose parameter is non-nullable. That's a CS8604 nullable warning, and this repo builds with warnings as errors.

At runtime, if a custom `IAccessTokenProvider` builds `new AccessTokenResult(RequiresRedirect, token, null, options)`, the URL is null. `NavigationManager.NavigateTo(null)` then throws a confusing `ArgumentNullException` instead of the old fallback.

The `else` branch also changed meaning. It used to navigate to the legacy `RedirectUrl`. Now it does `NavigateTo(InteractiveRequestUrl!)`, which skips `NavigateToLogin` and its options, and still throws when the URL is null.

::suggestion{file="src/Components/WebAssembly/WebAssembly.Authentication/src/Services/AccessTokenNotAvailableException.cs" lines="36-46"}
    public void Redirect()
    {
        if (_tokenResult.InteractiveRequestUrl is null)
        {
            throw new InvalidOperationException("The access token result does not contain an interactive request URL.");
        }

        if (_tokenResult.InteractionOptions != null)
        {
            _navigation.NavigateToLogin(_tokenResult.InteractiveRequestUrl, _tokenResult.InteractionOptions);
        }
        else
        {
            _navigation.NavigateTo(_tokenResult.InteractiveRequestUrl);
        }
    }

`AuthenticationManager_Logout_RedirectsToFailureOnInvalidSignOutState` set up a **non-null** history state with `Interaction = SignIn`. That case always went through `ValidateSignOutRequestState()`, never `SignOutManager`. The `testManager.SignOutState = false` line was irrelevant. The test still covers live behaviour: a logout request carrying a non-SignOut state must be rejected and sent to `logout-failed` with the 'not initiated from within the page' message.

::diff{file="src/Components/WebAssembly/WebAssembly.Authentication/test/RemoteAuthenticatorCoreTests.cs" lines="370-374"}

Restore the test without the `SignOutManager` lines. It now guards the only remaining CSRF check on logout.
